<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sacred kingship</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sacred kingship refers to the idea that a ruler possesses divine or supernatural qualities, or acts as an intermediary between the community and the spiritual world. In many African states it was a foundation of royal legitimacy and ritual. The profiles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luba-empire">Luba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> empires describe sacred kingship as central to their founding and governance, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> profiles also show rulers drawing authority from sacred status.</p></div>
